From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:14:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] windowing: variable refresh rate (FreeSync) for refresh rate
 changes

The Raspberry Pi 5 build's Kodi VRR support (its patches 0047-0050),
ported:

- with a vrr_capable connector and the CRTC's VRR_ENABLED property, set
  VRR_ENABLED with the mode (linux-9933 then sends FreeSync and switches
  rates without a modeset);
- modes that only differ in their vertical front porch from another of
  the same size are flagged D3DPRESENTFLAG_VRR; the ordinary mode a VRR
  mode of the same size and rate stands in for is left out;
- the whitelist search picks a VRR mode of a whitelisted size at the
  frame rate or twice it first (24p as 47.952 on a 40-120 Hz TV),
  except for native Dolby Vision, which keeps its CTA-861 modes;
  FindBestMatchingResolution prefers a VRR mode on ties;
- System > Display > "Variable refresh rate" (winsystem.vrr, shown on a
  VRR display; applies at the next flip), with its two strings in the
  fork's block (#45000, #45001). Off by default: none of this has run on
  hardware, on Intel (linux-9933, itself off by default) or on amdgpu's
  own FreeSync.

General in purpose, but it changes code the Dolby Vision series adds
(the native mode choice in the whitelist search, CDRMAtomic and
CWinSystemGbm), so it applies after it: it is numbered 9990, the range
of yblod/patches/kodi/ for general patches that need the Dolby Vision
series, after the series' 9950-9989.

Formerly yblod-36, with its strings as yblod-54 renumbered them.
